 '[{"text": "14", "feedback": "This adds the two side lengths (6 and 8) instead of using the distance formula on the diagonal itself."}, {"text": "10", "feedback": "Correct. AC = √((6 − 0)² + (8 − 0)²) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10."}, {"text": "48", "feedback": "This is 6 × 8, the rectangle''s area — not related to the length of a diagonal."}, {"text": "100", "feedback": "This is the value under the square root sign. One more step (taking the root) finishes the calculation."}]'::jsonb,
 'distance-formula'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'midpoint-and-length-of-a-line-segment', 40, 'Advanced',
 'Are the points A(1, 1), B(4, 5), and C(7, 9) collinear (do they lie on the same line)?', 0,
 '[{"text": "Yes, since AB + BC = AC (5 + 5 = 10)", "feedback": "Correct. AB = √(3²+4²) = 5, BC = √(3²+4²) = 5, and AC = √(6²+8²) = 10 — since AB + BC = AC, B lies exactly on segment AC, so all three points are collinear."}, {"text": "No, since AB ≠ BC ≠ AC", "feedback": "AB and BC actually come out equal here (both 5) — recompute both using the distance formula before comparing."}, {"text": "Yes, since AB = BC", "feedback": "AB and BC being equal only shows B is equally far from A and C — collinearity needs AB + BC to equal AC, which is a separate check."}, {"text": "No, not enough information to tell", "feedback": "The distance formula applied to all three pairs of points is exactly enough information to decide this."}]'::jsonb,
 'collinearity-via-distance'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'midpoint-and-length-of-a-line-segment', 41, 'Advanced',
 'Are the points A(0, 0), B(3, 4), and C(8, 2) collinear (do they lie on the same line)?', 2,
 '[{"text": "Yes, since AB + BC = AC", "feedback": "Check the actual values: AB = 5, BC ≈ 5.4, and AC ≈ 8.2 — their sum (≈10.4) doesn''t match AC, so this condition fails."}, {"text": "Yes, since AB = AC", "feedback": "AB (5) and AC (≈8.2) aren''t equal — and even if they were, that wouldn''t be the right test for collinearity."}, {"text": "No, since AB + BC ≠ AC", "feedback": "Correct. AB = 5, BC ≈ 5.4, and AC ≈ 8.2; since AB + BC ≈ 10.4 doesn''t equal AC, B does not lie on segment AC, so the three points aren''t collinear."}, {"text": "No, because none of the distances are whole numbers", "feedback": "Two of the three distances actually do come out to whole numbers here — and even if none did, that wouldn''t be the test for collinearity anyway."}]'::jsonb,
 'collinearity-via-distance'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'midpoint-and-length-of-a-line-segment', 42, 'Advanced',
 'The distance from (k, 3) to (5, 7) is 5. What are the two possible values of k?', 1,
 '[{"text": "k = 3 or k = 7", "feedback": "These reuse numbers already in the problem rather than solving √((5−k)² + 4²) = 5 for k."}, {"text": "k = 2 or k = 8", "feedback": "Correct. √((5−k)² + 4²) = 5 leads to (5−k)² = 9, so 5−k = ±3, giving k = 2 or k = 8."}, {"text": "k = 1 or k = 9", "feedback": "Close, but recheck (5−k)² = 5² − 4² = 9 — these values give a difference of 4 from 5, not 3."}, {"text": "k = 0 or k = 10", "feedback": "These give (5−k)² = 25, which is 5² itself — the 4² from the y-difference still needs to be subtracted first."}]'::jsonb,
 'distance-formula-solve-for-unknown'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'midpoint-and-length-of-a-line-segment', 43, 'Advanced',
 'A triangle has vertices A(0, 0), B(a, 0), and C(0, 3), where a > 0. If the triangle''s perimeter is 12, what is a?', 3,
 '[{"text": "a = 3", "feedback": "Check the perimeter with a = 3: sides 3, 3, and √18 ≈ 4.2, totalling about 10.2, not 12."}, {"text": "a = 6", "feedback": "Check the perimeter with a = 6: sides 6, 3, and √45 ≈ 6.7, totalling about 15.7, not 12."}, {"text": "a = 9", "feedback": "Check the perimeter with a = 9: sides 9, 3, and √90 ≈ 9.5, totalling well over 12."}, {"text": "a = 4", "feedback": "Correct. The perimeter is a + 3 + √(a²+9) = 12; isolating the root and squaring both sides gives a²+9 = 81−18a+a², so 18a = 72, and a = 4 — which gives the sides 4, 3, and 5."}]'::jsonb,
 'perimeter-solve-for-unknown-side'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'midpoint-and-length-of-a-line-segment', 44, 'Advanced',
 'Triangle ABC has vertices A(0, 0), B(10, 0), and C(4, 8). What is the length of the median from A to the midpoint of BC, to one decimal place?', 2,
 '[{"text": "7.0", "feedback": "Recheck the midpoint of BC first — it isn''t (7, 0); average both the x- and y-coordinates of B and C."}, {"text": "8.0", "feedback": "This looks like just the y-coordinate of C was used — find the actual midpoint of BC first, then its distance from A."}, {"text": "8.1", "feedback": "Correct. The midpoint of BC is ((10+4)÷2, (0+8)÷2) = (7, 4), and the distance from A(0,0) to (7,4) is √(7²+4²) = √65 ≈ 8.1."}, {"text": "11.0", "feedback": "This is close to BC''s own length, not the median''s — the median runs from A to BC''s midpoint, not along BC itself."}]'::jsonb,
 'median-length-via-midpoint'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'midpoint-and-length-of-a-line-segment', 45, 'Advanced',
 'Quadrilateral ABCD has A(0, 0), B(5, 1), C(9, 6), and D(4, 5). Verify it''s a parallelogram by checking that its diagonals share the same midpoint. What is that shared midpoint?', 1,
 '[{"text": "(4, 3)", "feedback": "Recompute the midpoint of diagonal AC using both A and C''s coordinates — this doesn''t match."}, {"text": "(4.5, 3)", "feedback": "Correct. The midpoint of AC is ((0+9)÷2, (0+6)÷2) = (4.5, 3), and the midpoint of BD is ((5+4)÷2, (1+5)÷2) = (4.5, 3) — the same point, confirming the diagonals bisect each other."}, {"text": "(5, 3.5)", "feedback": "This doesn''t match the midpoint of either diagonal — recompute using the actual coordinates of A, C and of B, D."}, {"text": "(9, 6)", "feedback": "This is just vertex C''s own coordinates, not the midpoint of a diagonal."}]'::jsonb,
