('MPM2D', 'solving-quadratic-equations', 'the-quadratic-formula', 1, 'Easy',
 'In the quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b²−4ac)) ÷ 2a, what is the expression b² − 4ac called?', 0,
 '[{"text": "The discriminant", "feedback": "Correct. b²−4ac is called the discriminant, and its sign tells you how many real solutions the equation has."}, {"text": "The vertex", "feedback": "The vertex is a point on a parabola''s graph — it isn''t the name of this particular expression inside the formula."}, {"text": "The axis of symmetry", "feedback": "The axis of symmetry is the vertical line x = −b÷2a — a different part of the quadratic formula."}, {"text": "The y-intercept", "feedback": "The y-intercept of a quadratic is simply c, found by substituting x = 0 — unrelated to this expression."}]'::jsonb,
 'discriminant-terminology'),
('MPM2D', 'solving-quadratic-equations', 'the-quadratic-formula', 2, 'Easy',
 'In the quadratic formula x = (-b ± √(b²-4ac)) ÷ 2a, what does the ''2a'' represent?', 1,
 '[{"text": "Part of the discriminant", "feedback": "The discriminant is the expression b²-4ac, under the square root — 2a is a separate part of the formula, outside the root."}, {"text": "The denominator of the whole expression", "feedback": "Correct. Everything above the fraction bar, -b ± √(b²-4ac), is divided by 2a — twice the leading coefficient."}, {"text": "The coefficient of x²", "feedback": "The coefficient of x² is just a on its own — 2a is that value doubled, used as the formula''s denominator."}, {"text": "The y-intercept", "feedback": "The y-intercept of a quadratic is c, found by substituting x = 0 — unrelated to the 2a in this formula."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadratic-formula-parts'),
('MPM2D', 'solving-quadratic-equations', 'the-quadratic-formula', 3, 'Easy',
 'For the equation x² - 5x + 6 = 0, what are the values of a, b, and c?', 0,
 '[{"text": "a = 1, b = -5, c = 6", "feedback": "Correct. Matching x²-5x+6=0 to ax²+bx+c=0 gives a=1 (the coefficient of x²), b=-5 (the coefficient of x), and c=6 (the constant term)."}, {"text": "a = 1, b = 5, c = 6", "feedback": "The sign of b is off — the coefficient of x here is -5, not 5."}, {"text": "a = -5, b = 1, c = 6", "feedback": "The values of a and b are swapped — a is the coefficient of x², and b is the coefficient of x."}, {"text": "a = 1, b = -5, c = -6", "feedback": "The sign of c is off — the constant term here is +6, not -6."}]'::jsonb,
 'identify-a-b-c'),
('MPM2D', 'solving-quadratic-equations', 'the-quadratic-formula', 4, 'Easy',
 'Use the quadratic formula to solve x² - 5x + 6 = 0.', 2,
 '[{"text": "x = 5 and x = 6", "feedback": "These reuse the equation''s own coefficients directly instead of applying the quadratic formula."}, {"text": "x = -2 and x = -3", "feedback": "The signs are flipped — recheck (-b ± √(b²-4ac)) ÷ 2a with b = -5, which makes -b positive."}, {"text": "x = 2 and x = 3", "feedback": "Correct. With a=1, b=-5, c=6: the discriminant is 25-24=1, so x = (5 ± 1) ÷ 2, giving x = 3 or x = 2."}, {"text": "x = 1 and x = 6", "feedback": "These don''t satisfy the original equation — recompute using a=1, b=-5, c=6 in the quadratic formula."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadratic-formula-basic-solve'),
('MPM2D', 'solving-quadratic-equations', 'the-quadratic-formula', 5, 'Easy',
 'For the equation 2x² + 3x - 2 = 0, what are the values of a, b, and c?', 1,
 '[{"text": "a = 2, b = 3, c = 2", "feedback": "The sign of c is off — the constant term here is -2, not 2."}, {"text": "a = 2, b = 3, c = -2", "feedback": "Correct. Matching 2x²+3x-2=0 to ax²+bx+c=0 gives a=2, b=3, and c=-2."}, {"text": "a = 3, b = 2, c = -2", "feedback": "The values of a and b are swapped — a is the coefficient of x², and b is the coefficient of x."}, {"text": "a = 2, b = -3, c = -2", "feedback": "The sign of b is off — the coefficient of x here is +3, not -3."}]'::jsonb,
 'identify-a-b-c'),
('MPM2D', 'solving-quadratic-equations', 'the-quadratic-formula', 6, 'Easy',
 'In the quadratic formula, what expression sits under the square root?', 0,
 '[
   {"text": "b² - 4ac", "feedback": "Correct."},
   {"text": "b² + 4ac", "feedback": "The 4ac SUBTRACTS. The sign under the root decides everything about the roots."},
   {"text": "4ac - b²", "feedback": "The b² leads. Reversed, the sign of the whole expression flips."},
   {"text": "b - 4ac", "feedback": "The b is squared under the root. Without the square the formula fails."}
 ]'::jsonb,
 null),
('MPM2D', 'solving-quadratic-equations', 'the-quadratic-formula', 7, 'Easy',
 'For 2x² - 5x + 1 = 0, what are a, b and c?', 0,
 '[
   {"text": "a = 2, b = -5, c = 1", "feedback": "Correct."},
   {"text": "a = 2, b = 5, c = 1", "feedback": "b carries its SIGN: the term is minus 5x, so b is -5."},
   {"text": "a = 2x², b = -5x, c = 1", "feedback": "a, b and c are the COEFFICIENTS only — the x parts stay behind."},
   {"text": "a = 1, b = -5, c = 2", "feedback": "a belongs to x² and c is the constant. They are swapped here."}
 ]'::jsonb,
 null),
('MPM2D', 'solving-quadratic-equations', 'the-quadratic-formula', 8, 'Easy',
 'A quadratic equation can have at most how many real solutions?', 2,
 '[
   {"text": "Unlimited", "feedback": "The degree of the equation caps how many roots exist."},
   {"text": "Three", "feedback": "A parabola cannot cross a horizontal line three times — count the crossings a U shape allows."},
   {"text": "Two", "feedback": "Correct."},
   {"text": "One", "feedback": "One happens only when the discriminant lands exactly on zero — it is not the ceiling."}
 ]'::jsonb,
 null),
('MPM2D', 'solving-quadratic-equations', 'the-quadratic-formula', 9, 'Medium',
 'How many times does   y = x² + 2x + 5   cross the x-axis?', 3,
 '[
   {"text": "Twice", "feedback": "Two crossings need a positive discriminant. Work out b² − 4ac before deciding."},
   {"text": "Once", "feedback": "One crossing happens only when the discriminant is exactly zero. Check the value of b² − 4ac here."},
   {"text": "Three times", "feedback": "A parabola is a single curve that turns once, so it can never meet a straight line three times."},
   {"text": "It does not cross at all", "feedback": "Correct. b² − 4ac = 4 − 20 = −16, and a negative discriminant means the curve never reaches the axis."}
 ]'::jsonb,
 'discriminant-sign-meaning'),
('MPM2D', 'solving-quadratic-equations', 'the-quadratic-formula', 10, 'Medium',
 'Use the quadratic formula to solve x² - 6x + 5 = 0.', 3,
